 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li><b>Chicken katsu</b>: shredded cabbage, a katsu, Dijon mustard and tonkatsu sauce.</li><li><b>Teriyaki salmon</b>, <b>bulgogi</b> and <b>Spam with egg</b> work the same way.</li><li>Any juicy cooked meat does: keep the filling flat-ish and thick in the middle, with something crisp (lettuce, cabbage) next to the sauce.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Other fillings (Just One Cookbook)"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A mold (about <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"9\" data-s=\"0\">9 cm</span> square) gives tidy edges.</li><li>Without one, just spread and stack carefully — the fold and the plastic wrap hold it together.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Mold or no mold"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Wrapped in plastic and then a thick kitchen towel, it keeps in the fridge overnight; the towel stops the rice going hard.</li><li>Take it out 30–60 minutes before eating.</li><li>Same day is best.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Making it ahead"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The batch of four, the ~<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"75\" data-s=\"0\">75 g</span> of rice per layer, the lettuce-and-pork amounts per sandwich and cooling the pork to just warm before building (hot filling steams the nori soft) are this site’s.</li><li>The build, the fold and the shogayaki are Just One Cookbook’s.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "This site’s additions"
  }
 ],
